A||||4||Ed||California Wildflowers II||||1||
Z||000000||Ed||03-22-2000||08:59 PM||||I'm back from a 700 mile drive around the central coast of California. <P>Listen folks, stop worrying about big brakes, big engines, paint jobs, and enjoy your cars. For you Californians: Carmel Valley road was 40 miles of twisty bits between Carmel (over by Monterey) and the famous Highway 101.  Only one holder-upper in this stretch.  The other unbelievable stretch was the one I've mentioned before: State Highway 25 south of Hollister. No pigs or deer on the road, and no cars either.  About 70 miles of sort of twisty bits, wildflowers, and green hills.  I only had to pass ONE car in 70 miles of a perfect sports car road. <P>I have a stock '87 GT. It never fails to give me all the driving fun I want/need. <P>So go out there and DRIVE, and have FUN with what you've got.  The Fiero is a great sports car, was relatively inexpensive to begin with, can be worked on at your GM dealers, doesn't need a mechanic named Guido or Hans, and doesn't rust (take THAT, Porsche).<P>I'm stoked: Go out tommorrow and drive your cars hard!   ||||reg||1
Z||000001||loafer87gt||03-23-2000||12:50 AM||||I totally hear you. Some of my best memories are flying down the twisties in my stock 87 GT on the way to our cabin up north. There is practically no traffic on the road, and there is tall trees on both sides. I can't help but grin ear to ear when I have the sunroof off and downshift to third and hear the sweet sounding exhaust. I will never sell this car, too many good memories.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||kevin||03-23-2000||01:54 AM||||Hey Ed. I was browsing around the Fiero news and read you are from Sacramento. Terrific, thought I would introduce myself. I am from Sacto. (C.H. actually)and LOVE Fiero's. Perhaps we should hook up sometime soon. Let me know. I'll await a reply. Good memories you have painted on a terrific drive. Kevin ||||reg||7
Z||000003||Robert||03-23-2000||02:41 PM||||One of my favorite roads to get to the mountains from the Bay Area is Highway 88.  Two lanes of rolling hills and tight turns.   It was on this road I had my first encounter with a Lamborghini Countach.  I was in my 88 Trans Am GTA.  I stayed up with him until we hit the first real turn, I didn’t even see his brake lights come on.  I came out of the turn, and he must have had a good 10 car lead.  Never saw him again.  Someday I hope to build a Fiero that will keep up with a Countach.||||reg||10
Z||000004||Ed||03-23-2000||08:50 PM||||I like Highway 88 too.  From Sacramento, Mormon Immigrant Trail, another good road, joins up with it a ways up the mountain. In fact, it was 88 that convinced me to get a six cylinder.<P>I was passed by a non-turbo Starion westbound on the east side of Carson Pass when I was in my first Fiero, an '84 SE. He went right on by and I was irritated.  <P>A couple of years later my '84 was rear-ended and totaled.  What to replace it with? Another Fiero! But the six.  I've never been sorry.  <P>Good luck on your upgrade, however.  Its pretty hard to compare a $15,000 car with a $100,000 car.  We do have more luggage space, though! ||||reg||1
